# =============================================================================
# Secrets - never commit
# =============================================================================
# .env holds a live GROQ_API_KEY. It was pasted in plaintext during development
# and must be rotated before any deploy; overwriting the file is the whole fix.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Streamlit's per-deploy credential file is the same hazard under another name.
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# =============================================================================
# Generated search index - COMMITTED, deliberately
# =============================================================================
# This used to be ignored, on the reasoning that the folder is reproducible from
# chunks.jsonl plus the model and is therefore an artefact rather than a
# deliverable. That reasoning was wrong about the deploy, and measurably so.
#
#   Streamlit Community Cloud's filesystem is EPHEMERAL. The container is
#   recycled, and a recycled container starts from the GitHub checkout again -
#   with no chroma_db/. So "rebuild it once at startup" is not "build it once",
#   it is "build it on every cold boot, forever", which is exactly what was
#   being avoided. Each one pays the ~90 MB model download and a fresh
#   377-chunk embed, and holds the 942 MB peak while it happens.
#
# 8.8 MB is a cheap price for removing that from every cold start. It is
# committed at the repo root because retrieval_engine resolves ROOT /
# "chroma_db". Note the folder is now a TRACKED artefact: a rebuild dirties the
# working tree, which is intended - a deployed index that silently differs from
# the committed one is worth seeing in `git status`.
#
# Shipping it is also safe by construction, not by hope. ensure_index() still
# rebuilds whenever index_status() cannot open a complete collection, so if
# these bytes ever fail to load on a platform this repo was not built on, the
# worst outcome is the old slow boot, never a broken app. Portability was
# checked rather than assumed: index_metadata.pickle contains only builtins and
# plain data (zero class references, so no library-version coupling),
# chroma.sqlite3 is ordinary SQLite, and the HNSW .bin files are
# little-endian, as is every platform this deploys to. chromadb is pinned to
# the exact version that wrote them.
#
# data/index/ is still ignored: nothing reads it, it is a leftover.
data/index/

# Same thing under a different name: a scratch index left behind while
# debugging the cold-start rebuild. No source file references it, so nothing
# reads it. The pattern is a glob rather than the bare name because the name
# varies with whatever probe was being run.
chroma_db_probe*/
chroma_db-*/
*_probe_db/

# =============================================================================
# Virtual environments
# =============================================================================
# 1.5 GB locally, and it must never be committed. The leading dot on .venv is
# not assumed - `venv/` and `env/` are the other two spellings people create by
# accident and then commit.
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# =============================================================================
# Python bytecode and caches
# =============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytype/

# =============================================================================
# Packaging
# =============================================================================
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
pip-wheel-metadata/

# =============================================================================
# Test, coverage and type artefacts
# =============================================================================
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/
.hypothesis/
.cache/

# =============================================================================
# Notebooks
# =============================================================================
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# =============================================================================
# Editors and operating systems
# =============================================================================
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# OneDrive sync artefacts. This tree lives under OneDrive/Documents, and
# OneDrive writes lock and conflict files that would otherwise show up as
# untracked noise on every git status.
~$*
*.tmp
*.lnk

# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED
# =============================================================================
# Listed because a broad rule would plausibly catch them, and each one is load
# bearing. See implementation.md.
#
#   data/processed/chunks.jsonl   committed source of truth for the cold-start
#                                 rebuild; if it is missing, ensure_index()
#                                 cannot work and the app cannot answer
#   data/processed/vectors.npy     the reference the bit-identical rebuild
#   data/processed/ids.json        assertion is measured against
#   data/manifest/run_log.jsonl    AC-11 is verified by READING this file, so
#                                 it is evidence, not a log to be rotated
#   data/chunks_preview.txt        a Phase 2 deliverable
#   prompts/system_prompt.v1.txt   versioned artefact with a drift test
#   config/sources.csv             scheme_id -> URL + tier
#
# In particular there is no `data/` rule and no `*.log` rule. A blanket `*.log`
# is a common way to accidentally hide run_log.jsonl's sibling evidence.
